@* Exercise 068 - Generic Component Basics (intermediate) - the call sites.
   No TODO here: this file is the demonstration the exercise is measured against.
   Read it - every badge but the last states no type argument at all, because the
   compiler infers T from Value. The last one has no Value to infer from, so T has
   to be given explicitly. The exercise itself is in
   Ex068_GenericComponentBasics_Badge.razor. *@
<div id="number">
    <Ex068_GenericComponentBasics_Badge Value="42" />
</div>
<div id="text">
    <Ex068_GenericComponentBasics_Badge Value="@Name" />
</div>
<div id="templated">
    @* The template's context is a string, not an object - that is inference reaching
       into the fragment. ToUpperInvariant would not compile if T were not string. *@
    <Ex068_GenericComponentBasics_Badge Value="@Name">
        <Template Context="text"><b>@text!.ToUpperInvariant()</b></Template>
    </Ex068_GenericComponentBasics_Badge>
</div>
<div id="explicit">
    <Ex068_GenericComponentBasics_Badge T="string" />
</div>
<div id="struct">
    @* Same shape, but T is a value type - so there is no null here to fall back on,
       and default(T) renders as the empty Guid. "T?" on an unconstrained T is an
       annotation, not Nullable<T>. *@
    <Ex068_GenericComponentBasics_Badge T="Guid" />
</div>

@code {
    [Parameter] public string Name { get; set; } = "ada";
}
